{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Generally, no. **Authorized participants (APs) aren’t typically paid by the ETF sponsor** for creating or redeeming shares. They’re usually broker-dealers that can transact directly with the fund, often in large creation units, and earn through trading economics—such as bid–ask spreads or arbitrage—while bearing transaction costs and risks. A sponsor may charge creation/redemption fees to cover costs.\n\n**APs aren’t obligated to keep creating or redeeming.** They may stop or reduce activity if it’s uneconomic or operationally difficult. Other APs may step in, but if participation dries up, ETF liquidity and price alignment with net asset value can suffer."}}
